*  GIT.VERSION — what is actually running.
*  Copyright (C) 2026 Gordon Heydon.  GPL-2.0-only (see LICENSE).
*
*  The build's version, libgit2's, and which of libgit2's optional transports
*  were compiled in.  SSH is the one that matters: whether an ssh remote can be
*  reached is a property of the libgit2 this was LINKED against, not of mv_git,
*  and with no way to ask, an ssh remote that could never work looks like a
*  credentials problem (mv_git#21).
*
*  ANSWERED BY WHATEVER IS RUNNING THE ENGINE.  On UniVerse that is mvgitd, a
*  separate binary from this verb which can drift from it — so the version that
*  comes back is the one actually doing the work, not the one asking.
*  NO ARGUMENT.  The framework dispatches every handler as `CALL @H` — no
*  arguments at all — so a handler that declares one is not callable by the
*  thing that calls it: `GIT VERSION` answered "0 argument(s) passed,
*  subroutine takes 1" (mv_git#85).  The sentence is reached the same way every
*  other handler reaches it, through the cmd framework, not through a parameter.
      SUBROUTINE GIT.VERSION
      $INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
      REPO = ""
      CALL GIT.REPO(REPO)
*     THE ONE QUESTION THAT NEEDS NO REPOSITORY, and it has to answer outside
*     one: `git --version` works in any directory, and the first thing anyone
*     does with a new install is ask what it is -- from an account that is not a
*     repository yet, which is exactly where this said
*
*         GIT: the engine did not answer VERSION
*
*     and read as a broken engine.  The engine was fine.  Off MVX the reply
*     comes back through a file under the path handed in, and GIT.REPO does not
*     answer "" when there is no repository -- it answers <account>/.git, a path
*     that does not exist.  So the engine wrote its reply into a directory that
*     was not there, the write failed silently, and nothing came back
*     (mv_git#215).
*
*     ASK WHETHER THE PATH IS REAL, the same way GIT.REPO's own walk does, and
*     fall back to the account: it is writable, it is where the verb is already
*     running, and VERSION reads nothing back from it -- it is somewhere to
*     leave a reply, not a repository being opened.
      RHEAD = "" ; RHOK = 0
      CALL GIT.OSFILE("READ", REPO : "/HEAD", RHEAD, RHOK)
      IF RHOK = 0 THEN REPO = "."
      VOUT = ""
      CALL GIT.OBJ("VERSION", 1, REPO, "", "", "", "", VOUT)
      IF VOUT = "" THEN
         CRT "GIT: the engine did not answer VERSION"
      END ELSE
         CALL GIT.ECHO(VOUT)
      END
      RETURN
      END
